        <p>
          This past February, five of our members (Abby Gerhardt, Bianca Leahu, Celestina Pint, Dania Hussein, and Rochelle Griffith) had the opportunity to attend the 2026 EWB-USA National Conference in Portland, Oregon! This is an annual conference where students, professionals, community partners, and other experts come together to discuss their experiences, form new connections, and learn how to improve our chapters and EWB-USA as a whole. Hundreds of attendees from around the country and the world travelled to Portland for an amazing weekend of community and conversation.
        </p>
        <a href="members"><img class="img-fluid rounded mb-4" src="images/conference-members.jpg" alt=""></a>
        <p>
        Each day of the conference, we attended a combination of small presentations, keynote speeches, and informal breakout sessions. Topics ranged widely from chapter operations to technical advice to ensuring sustainability and community input. The five of us attended different sets of presentations each day so that we could absorb as much information as possible. Here are some of our biggest takeaways.

        1. <b>We are not alone.</b> The challenges our chapter faces, from fundraising to membership, and from contractors to partner communication, are all common experiences. Chapters around the country have overcome these challenges in the past and continue to do so. Through the Annual Chapter Review, we saw that, even though these challenges seem large to us, our chapter is performing close to the average on fundraising, project costs, and other metrics. Gaining that perspective through EWB-wide data was very eye-opening.
        2. <b>Connections are a resource.</b> Almost everyone we talked to at the conference had valuable advice to offer. Not only were they rich in skills and knowledge, but they were eager and willing to provide guidance. We met a soil scientist from New Hampshire, a faculty mentor from Maryland, and even the president of EWB International, all of whom were excited to exchange knowledge and ideas. Other EWB volunteers are a resource that should not be underestimated!
        3. <b>Don’t underestimate the power of creativity in projects.</b> Through both project presentations and the poster session, we saw some amazing examples of creative solutions implemented by other chapters. For example, the University of Maryland chapter established a program for local technical college students to gain real-world experience through supporting project construction. As a result, both groups of students gained valuable knowledge, new relationships were fostered, and community ownership of the project was strengthened. We hope to take this out-of-the-box thinking as an inspiration for our own future projects.
        4. <b>EWB is full of people striving to make a positive impact.</b> It was reassuring and energizing to see how other chapters are pushing towards more sustainable and community-led projects. Students from the Cal Poly chapter gave a great presentation about the history of international development and how it should play into the work that we do. Some of the most meaningful sessions were those in which we engaged in discussion about the role of EWB. Are the learning goals and the project goals of EWB in conflict? How do we move from community consultation to community ownership? These are all crucial questions that we plan to continue discussing as a chapter.

        We came back to Cambridge with lots of inspiration and momentum to improve how we function as a team. Hopefully, all that we learned will help us be more successful and effective as a chapter in the future. We are looking forward to next year’s conference and all the lessons we have yet to learn, as well as those we will impart to others!        
        </p>
